From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stage C <stage-c@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 22:05:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: address constant aggregate locals in place

cg_clif materialises every `_tmp = const ARRAY` assignment as a stack slot and
a full `memcpy`. For a constant array that is only read, such as the 256-byte
K table that SHA-256 indexes in every round, that copies the whole table into
the shadow stack per iteration: `emit_small_memory_copy` gives up above four
accesses and calls `compiler_builtins`' `copy_forward`, which is 79% of
sha256's executed instructions.

Find locals whose only write is one constant aggregate assignment and that are
never address-taken, dropped or interior-mutable, and make their place point
at the constant's read-only data allocation instead. The initialising
assignment is then a no-op. Terminator writes (`call`/`tail_call`
destinations, `drop` places, coroutine `yield` resume arguments) and inline
asm outputs are rejected as well as statement writes, so the alias can never
be written through.

This is cg_clif's version of LLVM's store-to-load forwarding for this shape;
the MIR itself is valid and rustc has no constant-place representation to use
in its place. On sha256 the K-table copy disappears: `copy_forward` is no
longer executed at all and the module's instruction count drops from 363.0M to
71.5M (8.57x to 1.69x LLVM -O2) in bench/gap.
